{"name":"Chocobo Feather","hex":"#f9bc08","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chocobo","blocks":[{"text":"Chocobo are a fictional species created for the Final Fantasy franchise by Square Enix. A galliform bird commonly having yellow feathers, they were first introduced in Final Fantasy II (1988), and have since featured in some capacity in nearly every Final Fantasy title, usually as a means of transport. Chocobos or chocobo-themed characters have played story roles in multiple titles, notably in Final Fantasy V and XIII. A recurring Chocobo also acts as protagonist of the Chocobo spin-off series."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/67/Chocobo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chocobo"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feather"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Parts of a feather: VaneShaft, rachisBarbsAftershaft, afterfeatherQuill, calamus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParts_of_feather_modified.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pigeon_down_SEM.tif/lossless-page1-500px-Pigeon_down_SEM.tif.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anterior region of a contour feather from a rock dove, visualized using scanning electron microscopy. Interlocking barbules are clearly seen in the middle image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APigeon_down_SEM.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg/500px-Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg/1280px-Grand_Cormoran_%28Phalacrocorax_carbo%29%2C_Parc_de_Woluw%C3%A9%2C_Bruxelles_%2850915141282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org